To solve for the vector \(\mathbf{v}\) in the equation \((\mathbf{A}^8 + \mathbf{A}^6 + \mathbf{A}^4 + \mathbf{A}^2 + \mathbf{I}) \mathbf{v} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 11 \end{pmatrix}\), we start by examining the properties of the matrix \(\mathbf{A}\).

First, we calculate powers of \(\mathbf{A}\):
\[
\mathbf{A} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 \\ 3 & 0 \end{pmatrix}
\]
\[
\mathbf{A}^2 = \mathbf{A} \cdot \mathbf{A} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 \\ 3 & 0 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 \\ 3 & 0 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 3 & 0 \\ 0 & 3 \end{pmatrix} = 3 \mathbf{I}
\]
\[
\mathbf{A}^3 = \mathbf{A} \cdot \mathbf{A}^2 = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 \\ 3 & 0 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} 3 & 0 \\ 0 & 3 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 3 \\ 9 & 0 \end{pmatrix} = 9 \mathbf{A}
\]
\[
\mathbf{A}^4 = \mathbf{A} \cdot \mathbf{A}^3 = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 \\ 3 & 0 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 3 \\ 9 & 0 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 27 & 0 \\ 0 & 27 \end{pmatrix} = 27 \mathbf{I}
\]
From these calculations, we observe a pattern:
\[
\mathbf{A}^n = \begin{cases} 
3^n I & \text{if } n \text{ is odd} \\ 
27 \cdot 3^{(n-2) / 2} I & \text{if } n \text{ is even} 
\end{cases}
\]
Thus, we can simplify the expression \(\mathbf{A}^2 + \mathbf{A}^4 + \mathbf{A}^6 + \mathbf{A}^8\):
\[
\mathbf{A}^2 = 3 \mathbf{I}
\]
\[
\mathbf{A}^4 = 27 \mathbf{I}
\]
\[
\mathbf{A}^6 = 3^3 \mathbf{I} = 27 \mathbf{I}
\]
\[
\mathbf{A}^8 = 3^4 \mathbf{I} = 81 \mathbf{I}
\]
Adding these together:
\[
\mathbf{A}^2 + \mathbf{A}^4 + \mathbf{A}^6 + \mathbf{A}^8 = 3 \mathbf{I} + 27 \mathbf{I} + 27 \mathbf{I} + 81 \mathbf{I} = 138 \mathbf{I}
\]
Therefore, the equation becomes:
\[
(\mathbf{A}^8 + \mathbf{A}^6 + \mathbf{A}^4 + \mathbf{A}^2 + \mathbf{I}) \mathbf{v} = 138 \mathbf{I} \mathbf{v} = 138 \mathbf{v} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 11 \end{pmatrix}
\]
Solving for \(\mathbf{v}\):
\[
\mathbf{v} = \frac{1}{138} \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 11 \end{pmatrix}
\]
Thus, the final answer is:
\[
\boxed{\begin{pmatrix} \frac{1}{138} \\ \frac{11}{138} \end{pmatrix}}
\]
